# valibot-to-openapi — project map

A zero-dependency TypeScript library that builds OpenAPI 3.0 / 3.1 / 3.2 documents from
[Valibot](https://valibot.dev/) schemas. Published to npm as `valibot-to-openapi`.
The only runtime and type dependency is `valibot` itself (a peer dependency).

## Workspace layout

pnpm workspace driven by [vite-plus](https://vite.plus) (`vp`), declared in `pnpm-workspace.yaml`:

| Path                          | Package                       | Role                                                                 |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `packages/valibot-to-openapi` | `valibot-to-openapi`          | The published library. All product code and unit tests live here.     |
| `test`                        | `@valibot-to-openapi/test`    | Private end-to-end workspace: generates documents into `test/out/`.   |
| `example`                     | `@valibot-to-openapi/example` | Private runnable example used in the docs.                            |
| `scripts`                     | —                             | `actionlint.ts`: downloads and runs a checksum-pinned actionlint.      |

`test` and `example` consume the library through `workspace:^`, which resolves to
`packages/valibot-to-openapi/dist`. Both need the library built before they run.

`README.md` at the repository root is a **symlink** to `packages/valibot-to-openapi/README.md`.
Edit the file under `packages/`, never the symlink.

## Library source layout

Everything under `packages/valibot-to-openapi/src`. One directory per layer, one `index.ts`
entry per directory, tests as `*.test.ts` next to the code they cover.

```text
src/
  index.ts          public entry: `export * from 'valibot'` plus this package's exports
  openapi/          the OpenAPI object model (readonly types only)
  utils/            generic helpers and type guards
  errors/           the error union and its constructors
  guard/            structural views over valibot schema objects
  types/            the library's own types; re-exports the openapi model
  pipe/             reading `v.pipe` chains (actions, wrappers, defaults)
  metadata/         the `openapi()` action and metadata collection
  specifics/        per-OpenAPI-version behavior (3.0 vs 3.1 vs 3.2)
  generator/        per-schema-type transformers, pure
  helper/           composition with the generation context
  core/             public entry points (registry, generateDocument, class wrappers)
```

## Everyday commands

Run from the repository root:

```bash
vp install                                        # install (add --frozen-lockfile in CI)
vp run --filter ./packages/valibot-to-openapi build  # build dist/ (needed by test + example)
vp run -r test                                    # vitest across the workspace
vp run check                                      # format check + oxlint (type-aware) everywhere
vp run lint                                       # markdownlint, textlint, cspell, secretlint, actionlint
vp run fix                                        # auto-fix formatting, Markdown, prose and lint
vp run generate                                   # build, then write test/out/*.yaml
vp run example                                    # build, then run the example script
```

`vp run check` is this repository's single definition of "clean": CI runs exactly the commands
above, in that order, so a green local run and a green CI run cannot disagree.

## Hard rules

1. **Nothing throws.** Every fallible function returns `{ ok: true, value } | { ok: false, error }`
   inline. There is no named `Result` type and no error class.
2. **No `any`, no type assertions** in `src` — `typescript/consistent-type-assertions` is set to
   `assertionStyle: 'never'`.
3. **No `let`** outside a `for` head, and module-level functions are `function` declarations.
4. **Return types are inferred**, not written, unless recursion forces an annotation.
5. **The lint configuration is strict on purpose.** Never relax a rule to make code pass; fix the
   code. See `lint-config` rule.
